WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.305 --> 00:00:03.180
SETH VARGO: Hi there.

00:00:03.180 --> 00:00:06.660
And welcome to our 10th
episode on SRE and DevOps.

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:08.520
My name is Seth and I'm
a developer advocate

00:00:08.520 --> 00:00:10.957
at Google focused on
infrastructure and operations.

00:00:10.957 --> 00:00:13.290
LIZ FONG-JONES: And I'm Liz,
a site reliability engineer

00:00:13.290 --> 00:00:15.870
at Google where I teach Google
Cloud customers how to build

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:18.310
and operate reliable services.

00:00:18.310 --> 00:00:20.084
So I've just wrapped
up an incident.

00:00:20.084 --> 00:00:22.500
And I definitely don't want
to have the same set of things

00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:23.940
happen in the exact same ways.

00:00:23.940 --> 00:00:25.360
What should I do to learn?

00:00:25.360 --> 00:00:27.943
SETH VARGO: So Liz, you want to
conduct a blameless postmortem

00:00:27.943 --> 00:00:29.070
or retrospective.

00:00:29.070 --> 00:00:31.860
There are a few key things that
go into every retrospective,

00:00:31.860 --> 00:00:35.610
but there are a variety of ways
of collecting that information.

00:00:35.610 --> 00:00:38.070
First, you'll need
some basic metadata,

00:00:38.070 --> 00:00:41.040
like what systems were
affected and what humans

00:00:41.040 --> 00:00:42.540
were involved in that process.

00:00:42.540 --> 00:00:42.780
LIZ FONG-JONES: Right.

00:00:42.780 --> 00:00:45.510
It doesn't make sense to conduct
a postmortem without involving

00:00:45.510 --> 00:00:46.740
everyone who played a part.

00:00:46.740 --> 00:00:47.490
SETH VARGO: Right.

00:00:47.490 --> 00:00:50.010
And then you need to start
assembling any records

00:00:50.010 --> 00:00:53.730
that you have and start to
begin understanding a timeline,

00:00:53.730 --> 00:00:56.730
like, how did we find
out about the event?

00:00:56.730 --> 00:00:58.930
When did we start responding?

00:00:58.930 --> 00:01:01.380
What mitigations did we
deploy, either temporarily

00:01:01.380 --> 00:01:02.880
or permanently?

00:01:02.880 --> 00:01:05.161
When did the incident
actually conclude?

00:01:05.161 --> 00:01:06.660
And it's important
to note that this

00:01:06.660 --> 00:01:08.550
is a collaborative process.

00:01:08.550 --> 00:01:11.940
Initially, the draft is written
by the incident commanders

00:01:11.940 --> 00:01:14.310
but all participants
collaborate and add

00:01:14.310 --> 00:01:16.150
their relevant information.

00:01:16.150 --> 00:01:18.150
It's usually helpful to
use a collaborative tool

00:01:18.150 --> 00:01:19.444
like Google Docs for this step.

00:01:19.444 --> 00:01:21.360
LIZ FONG-JONES: I'm
starting to understand now

00:01:21.360 --> 00:01:24.107
why you emphasized the role of
the planning lead last time.

00:01:24.107 --> 00:01:26.190
It's definitely a lot
easier to [? coord ?] things

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:29.130
as you go rather than having
to piece everything together

00:01:29.130 --> 00:01:30.030
after the fact.

00:01:30.030 --> 00:01:31.890
SETH VARGO: And you'll also need
to understand what things you

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:34.140
did that you'll need to
roll back or clean up

00:01:34.140 --> 00:01:36.900
afterwards, things like
temporarily increasing

00:01:36.900 --> 00:01:39.420
the size of a VM or directing
traffic away from a zone.

00:01:39.420 --> 00:01:40.920
LIZ FONG-JONES: I
can definitely see

00:01:40.920 --> 00:01:43.294
how it could get expensive or
risky if I didn't put those

00:01:43.294 --> 00:01:44.670
back the way that I found them.

00:01:44.670 --> 00:01:46.690
SETH VARGO: But most
importantly in the process,

00:01:46.690 --> 00:01:49.520
though, is making sure that
you have a blameless culture,

00:01:49.520 --> 00:01:52.472
ensuring that people feel like
they won't be faulted or fired

00:01:52.472 --> 00:01:54.180
for the mistakes that
they may have made.

00:01:54.180 --> 00:01:55.680
LIZ FONG-JONES: I
definitely see how

00:01:55.680 --> 00:01:58.170
I'd be really reluctant
to say, I messed up,

00:01:58.170 --> 00:02:00.630
if I were afraid of
being fired or ridiculed.

00:02:00.630 --> 00:02:01.600
SETH VARGO: Exactly.

00:02:01.600 --> 00:02:03.180
And participating
in a postmortem

00:02:03.180 --> 00:02:06.130
is a learning opportunity
for everyone involved,

00:02:06.130 --> 00:02:08.990
including the company, not
some kind of punishment.

00:02:08.990 --> 00:02:10.410
And it works best
if your team can

00:02:10.410 --> 00:02:14.040
think about the possibility
of a well-intentioned human

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:17.430
to cause a system failure
as a bug in the system,

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:18.950
not as a bug in the human.

00:02:18.950 --> 00:02:19.866
LIZ FONG-JONES: Right.

00:02:19.866 --> 00:02:20.880
You can't fix humans.

00:02:20.880 --> 00:02:23.338
SETH VARGO: It's often helpful
to ask questions like, "what

00:02:23.338 --> 00:02:26.280
did you know at the time"
or "how did the system

00:02:26.280 --> 00:02:29.760
behave" as opposed to "why
did you restart the process"

00:02:29.760 --> 00:02:32.160
in order to avoid putting
people on the defensive.

00:02:32.160 --> 00:02:32.430
LIZ FONG-JONES: Right.

00:02:32.430 --> 00:02:34.500
So how do you actually
organize this information

00:02:34.500 --> 00:02:35.760
once you're
collecting it though?

00:02:35.760 --> 00:02:36.870
SETH VARGO: So
every organization

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:38.610
has different ways
for communicating.

00:02:38.610 --> 00:02:40.110
As I said above,
at Google, we tend

00:02:40.110 --> 00:02:41.970
to use Google Docs and comments.

00:02:41.970 --> 00:02:43.770
Other organizations
may prefer something

00:02:43.770 --> 00:02:45.210
like a live conference
bridge with someone

00:02:45.210 --> 00:02:46.650
taking notes in the background.

00:02:46.650 --> 00:02:46.980
LIZ FONG-JONES: Right.

00:02:46.980 --> 00:02:47.736
That makes sense.

00:02:47.736 --> 00:02:49.110
And you reminded
me by mentioning

00:02:49.110 --> 00:02:52.050
taking notes during an
audio only conference bridge

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:54.600
that it's not really a great
idea to, during an incident,

00:02:54.600 --> 00:02:56.820
having audio only conference
bridges because you have

00:02:56.820 --> 00:02:58.350
to have someone
record very detailed

00:02:58.350 --> 00:03:00.810
notes in order to reconstruct
what happened after the fact.

00:03:00.810 --> 00:03:02.476
But we haven't really
gotten to anything

00:03:02.476 --> 00:03:05.094
about how do we prevent the
root cause from happening again.

00:03:05.094 --> 00:03:06.760
SETH VARGO: Well, be
careful there, Liz.

00:03:06.760 --> 00:03:09.180
Remember that failures in
distributed systems almost

00:03:09.180 --> 00:03:11.340
never have a single root cause.

00:03:11.340 --> 00:03:13.680
It's usually more than
one contributing factor

00:03:13.680 --> 00:03:15.570
that's causing an
intersection or some kind

00:03:15.570 --> 00:03:16.950
of cascading failure.

00:03:16.950 --> 00:03:19.380
So you'll want to write down
everything that contributed

00:03:19.380 --> 00:03:21.600
to this abnormal behavior
instead of just trying

00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.572
to identify the root cause.

00:03:23.572 --> 00:03:25.530
LIZ FONG-JONES: So what
I should be focusing on

00:03:25.530 --> 00:03:28.740
is how do I mitigate
individual kinds of failures

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:30.900
and how do I prevent
them from intersecting

00:03:30.900 --> 00:03:32.100
in these same ways.

00:03:32.100 --> 00:03:32.850
SETH VARGO: Right.

00:03:32.850 --> 00:03:35.460
And then you'll want to keep
track of that is explicitly

00:03:35.460 --> 00:03:37.680
filing an issue in your
issue tracker of choice

00:03:37.680 --> 00:03:39.600
for each action
item so that you'll

00:03:39.600 --> 00:03:41.652
make sure to prioritize
it for the future.

00:03:41.652 --> 00:03:43.860
LIZ FONG-JONES: And I can
definitely tag these things

00:03:43.860 --> 00:03:46.350
with the postmortem
tag in my bug tracker

00:03:46.350 --> 00:03:49.380
so I can see how at risk I am
of repeat outages based off

00:03:49.380 --> 00:03:51.870
of how many high priority
items that I've checked off

00:03:51.870 --> 00:03:53.550
and how many remain outstanding.

00:03:53.550 --> 00:03:54.390
SETH VARGO: Exactly.

00:03:54.390 --> 00:03:57.540
And you'll also want to capture
some overall themes like what

00:03:57.540 --> 00:04:00.420
went well, what went
poorly, and, honestly, what

00:04:00.420 --> 00:04:02.002
did we just get lucky with.

00:04:02.002 --> 00:04:03.960
LIZ FONG-JONES: I also
imagine that recognizing

00:04:03.960 --> 00:04:05.420
people that went
above and beyond

00:04:05.420 --> 00:04:07.800
and systems that worked
well and worked as designed

00:04:07.800 --> 00:04:09.937
is a good positive
motivator as well.

00:04:09.937 --> 00:04:10.770
SETH VARGO: Exactly.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:12.590
And one last key thing
to keep track of,

00:04:12.590 --> 00:04:14.850
once you understand the
incident in its entirety,

00:04:14.850 --> 00:04:17.574
is to write down machine
readable metadata

00:04:17.574 --> 00:04:18.990
so that you can
track improvements

00:04:18.990 --> 00:04:21.510
to your overall incident
response management process

00:04:21.510 --> 00:04:25.140
over time, such as decreasing
the total time to resolve

00:04:25.140 --> 00:04:27.990
incidents, or knowing whether
a release was involved,

00:04:27.990 --> 00:04:31.470
or a cascading failure, or
config changes, et cetera.

00:04:31.470 --> 00:04:33.990
This can help you identify
meta patterns in your outages

00:04:33.990 --> 00:04:36.210
and build either
process or technology

00:04:36.210 --> 00:04:38.830
to help prevent or mitigate
those incidents in the future.

00:04:38.830 --> 00:04:41.121
LIZ FONG-JONES: And that's
where Google statistics come

00:04:41.121 --> 00:04:43.080
from that approximately
70% of our outages

00:04:43.080 --> 00:04:45.000
are from binary
configuration changes,

00:04:45.000 --> 00:04:46.830
because we've been
recording that metadata

00:04:46.830 --> 00:04:47.914
over the past seven years.

00:04:47.914 --> 00:04:49.288
SETH VARGO:
Hopefully, these tips

00:04:49.288 --> 00:04:51.330
help you prepare for
your next incident.

00:04:51.330 --> 00:04:53.940
And thanks to everyone for
watching this series so far.

00:04:53.940 --> 00:04:55.440
Please write to us
on Twitter if you

00:04:55.440 --> 00:04:58.450
have feedback or suggestions
for future topics.

00:04:58.450 --> 00:04:59.580
Thank you for watching.

00:04:59.580 --> 00:05:02.290
Please be sure to like
and subscribe below.

00:05:02.290 --> 00:05:05.640
